= 10 October 2023 — Two resignations, five days out

General **Rajmund Andrzejczak**, Chief of the General Staff, and General
**Tomasz Piotrowski**, Operational Commander of the Armed Forces, resign
together five days before the parliamentary election. Neither man gives a
public reason beyond the impossibility of continuing. The defence ministry
briefs that the departures are personal; officers brief that the command has
spent months being contradicted in public by political appointees while a war
runs along the border.

The Constitution keeps the armed forces neutral and under civilian control,
which is exactly why this is dangerous in both directions. A government may
lawfully lose confidence in its generals. A government five days from an
election, on a frontier state's eastern flank, with no successor named and no
explanation offered, has produced a hole in the chain of command that no
campaign message closes.

[? if left_in_government = 1: We share cabinet responsibility, so our defence
and interior people will be asked what they knew and when, and any answer that
sounds like a party line will be quoted for years.?][? if left_in_government =
0: We hold no portfolio and no access to the operational picture. We can demand
the record, protect the officers from becoming a campaign prop, or make this
the closing argument about competence.?]

[? if opposition_infosec_committee = 1: The joint opposition committee that has
been meeting since the 2021 mailbox leak is the only forum in which three
caucuses can sign one security document inside a week.?]
[? if security_incident_pressure >= 10: This lands on a security record that
already includes the missile at Przewodów and the border emergency, and the
public has stopped treating each of them as isolated.?]

- @defence_command_record
- @defence_command_campaign
- @defence_command_neutral
- @defence_command_joint

@defence_command_record
title: Demand the written record and a named successor before polling day
subtitle: Treats the vacancy as an operational emergency; refuses to trade the chain of command for five days of advantage.
on-arrival: {!
Q.defence_command_response = "Operational record demanded";
Q.civil_military_trust =
    (Number(Q.civil_military_trust) || 36) + 10;
Q.defence_readiness = (Number(Q.defence_readiness) || 42) + 4;
Q.institutional_trust = (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) + 4;
Q.constitutional_restraint =
    (Number(Q.constitutional_restraint) || 0) + 4;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 2;
Q.pis_relation = (Number(Q.pis_relation) || 0) - 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left asks who is commanding the eastern flank on Sunday";
!}

We put one question in writing to the ministry and the Palace: who holds
operational command between now and the first sitting of the new Sejm, and
under what order. The question is procedural, boring and unanswerable by a
press officer, which is why it is the only version of this that officers
themselves can support.

The generals stay out of the campaign. So do we, on this subject, and the
professional part of the defence establishment notices which party treated a
command vacancy as a security problem rather than a gift.

- @poland_event_queue: File the command question

@defence_command_campaign
title: Make the resignations the closing argument about competence
subtitle: The strongest available attack five days out; it recruits the officer corps into an election and they will remember being used.
on-arrival: {!
Q.defence_command_response = "Closing competence attack";
Q.civil_military_trust = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.civil_military_trust) || 36) - 8
);
Q.left_poll_momentum = (Number(Q.left_poll_momentum) || 0) + 0.3;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 2;
Q.pis_relation = (Number(Q.pis_relation) || 0) - 8;
Q.institutional_trust = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) - 2
);
Q.security_incident_pressure =
    (Number(Q.security_incident_pressure) || 0) + 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "The command crisis becomes the opposition's last campaign argument";
!}

Eight years of defence spending announcements end with the two officers who
would actually run a war walking out in the final week. The argument writes
itself, it is true, and it moves voters who had stopped listening to anything
else we said.

It also drags serving officers into a partisan fight they cannot answer, and
sets the precedent that a command resignation is campaign material. Whoever
governs next inherits a general staff that has learned what its resignations
are worth in votes.

- @poland_event_queue: Take the command crisis to the closing rallies

@defence_command_neutral
title: Ask the President and the ministry for a joint continuity statement
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Buys an institutional answer at the price of sharing the credit with the people who caused the vacancy.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: No senior channel is open to broker a continuity statement in five days.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources = (Number(Q.resources) || 0) - 1;
Q.defence_command_response = "Brokered continuity statement";
Q.civil_military_trust =
    (Number(Q.civil_military_trust) || 36) + 14;
Q.defence_readiness = (Number(Q.defence_readiness) || 42) + 6;
Q.president_relation = (Number(Q.president_relation) || 0) + 4;
Q.institutional_trust = (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) + 5;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 1;
Q.left_poll_momentum = (Number(Q.left_poll_momentum) || 0) - 0.1;
Q.news_headline =
    "Palace and ministry publish a joint command-continuity statement";
!}

The Palace holds supreme command in peacetime through the defence minister,
and that is the door we use. A short joint statement names who exercises
operational command until a successor is appointed, and commits both offices
to the appointment inside a stated period.

Voters barely register it. The eastern-flank allies who spent the weekend
asking questions do, and so does every officer who wanted to know whether the
political class could still produce one sentence together about the army.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the continuity statement

@defence_command_joint
title: Take it to the joint opposition security committee and publish one demand
subtitle: Requires the standing joint committee. Removes the vacancy from the campaign by putting every democratic caucus's name on the same paper.
choose-if: opposition_infosec_committee = 1
unavailable-subtitle: There is no standing joint security forum — building one in five days, in the last week of a campaign, is not possible.
on-arrival: {!
Q.defence_command_response = "Joint opposition demand";
Q.civil_military_trust =
    (Number(Q.civil_military_trust) || 36) + 12;
Q.defence_readiness = (Number(Q.defence_readiness) || 42) + 5;
Q.institutional_trust = (Number(Q.institutional_trust) || 0) + 6;
Q.ko_relation = (Number(Q.ko_relation) || 0) + 4;
Q.p2050_relation = (Number(Q.p2050_relation) || 0) + 3;
Q.constitutional_restraint =
    (Number(Q.constitutional_restraint) || 0) + 4;
Q.public_trust = (Number(Q.public_trust) || 0) + 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "Three opposition caucuses sign one demand on the command vacancy";
!}

The committee meets on the Wednesday and publishes on the Thursday: one page,
three signatures, four questions about operational command between now and the
first sitting. It is the only document produced in that week by anybody that a
serving officer could have written.

Because it is joint, it cannot be dismissed as a campaign stunt by any single
party, and because it exists at all, the incoming government inherits a
cross-party text on command continuity instead of a quarrel about who
politicised the army.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the joint demand
